## How to Keep Short Almond Nails Looking Salon-Fresh at Home

Short almond is one of the lower-maintenance shapes shorter nails have fewer stress points and break less frequently than longer ones but a small routine keeps them looking deliberate rather than grown-out.

File in one direction only. The back-and-forth sawing motion that most people default to creates micro-fractures in the nail plate over time, weakening the free edge and increasing the risk of splitting. One smooth stroke from the side of the nail toward the centre, on both sides, is all the maintenance shape needs between salon visits. The full technique is covered in the <a href='/blog/how-to-shape-almond-nails'>how to shape almond nails at home guide</a>.

Cuticle oil daily not occasionally. The cuticle is the seal between the nail plate and the surrounding skin; when it dries, the nail plate itself becomes more brittle. A small amount of cuticle oil pressed into the base of each nail each evening takes thirty seconds and extends both nail health and polish longevity by a measurable margin. <a href='https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7105659/' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Nail health research</a> supports consistent hydration as one of the most effective interventions for reducing brittleness and breakage.

Top coat every three days if wearing regular polish. A thin refresh coat applied over the existing colour without removing it seals any hairline chips at the edges and restores the finish. With BIAB or a gel overlay, the three-day rule applies to cuticle oil only; the overlay itself needs no refreshing between appointments.

