## What to Tell Your Nail Tech to Get the Perfect Short Almond Shape

The salon brief matters more than most people realise. "Almond" without further instruction often returns something longer or more tapered than the reader wanted. A precise ask produces a precise result.

Ask for: a short almond at natural length, with a free edge of two to three millimetres. Specify that you want the sides tapered but not aggressively filed a gentle slope, not a sharp angle. If you want the shape as close to the fingertip as possible, "micro almond" or "baby almond" will land correctly with most technicians.

For clients who want added strength: ask specifically for BIAB (Builder in a Bottle) rather than a standard gel top coat. BIAB is thicker, more flexible, and adds structural support without the visual bulk of a full acrylic extension. It is the preferred route for anyone growing their natural nails into the shape, or for anyone whose nails tend to peel or break at the free edge.

Bring a reference image. The clearest brief is still a photograph one that shows the length, the taper angle, and the finish you want. Most nail technicians will replicate a reference more accurately than they will interpret a verbal description, however precise.

